<node id="FINDING::PSI-25_title">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Burning and Suppression of Solids - II</data>
  <data key="d13">title: Burning and Suppression of Solids - II</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-25_title</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-25_objective">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Burning and Suppression of Solids - II</data>
  <data key="d13">objective: BASS-II results contribute to the combustion computational models used in the design of fire detection and suppression systems in microgravity and on Earth. Objectives Include: 1) Study the ignition, flame growth, flame spread, and extinction limits for solid fuels burning in low-velocity forced flows in microgravity. 2) Begin to bridge the gap between the normal gravity NASA-STD-6001 Test #1 method, ground-based microgravity tests, and actual material flammability in microgravity. 3) Provide SoFIE PIs with preliminary data to refine Science Requirements. 4) Practical, realistic (thicker) fuels in typical geometries will be examined, including slabs, cylinders, and spherical sections. The primary variables include: • Forced flow velocity (speed and direction) • Ambient oxygen concentration (via working volume nitrogen vitiation) • Sample geometry (rods, spherical section, slabs, films, and fabric sheets</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-25_objective</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-25_hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Burning and Suppression of Solids - II</data>
  <data key="d13">hypothesis: BASS–II tests the hypothesis that materials in microgravity, with adequate ventilation, burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical (pressure, O2 concentration, temperature, etc.). NASA tests and controls materials from a flammability perspective using an upward burning test in normal gravity, which is considered to be the worst-case geometry for flammability of the material on Earth. However, it may not be conservative for flammability in space. Testing at constant O2 mole fractions permits studying flame spread and extinction at low and high flow speed. We are interested in finding the fundamental limit for a given material.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-25_hypothesis</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-25_research_area">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Burning and Suppression of Solids - II</data>
  <data key="d13">research_area: Combustion Science</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-25_research_area</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-25_research_impacts">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Burning and Suppression of Solids - II</data>
  <data key="d13">research_impacts: A primary goal of BASS-II is improved spacecraft fire safety, improved understanding of combustion in space and how to avoid it. If you’re on a mission far from Earth, a fire can be catastrophic. BASS-II helps engineers select the safest materials and improve firefighting methods on future space missions. In outer space, fuels burn as oval balls rather than with an upward pointed cone flame as they do on Earth. This simpler combustion process enables researchers to evaluate computer models of fuel burning. These models can then be used to more accurately study flames on Earth, such as in wildfires, building fires, energy recapture from waste recycling, and other combustion problems.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-25_research_impacts</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-69_title">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Flame Extinguishment Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">title: Flame Extinguishment Experiment</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-69_title</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-69_objective">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Flame Extinguishment Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">objective: The FLEX (Flame Extinguishment Experiment) investigation aims to map the flammability limits of liquid fuels in microgravity environments, measure the effectiveness of various gaseous fire suppressants such as CO2, He, and SF6, and develop reliable predictive models for combustion limits under reduced gravity conditions. The mission seeks to validate and improve simplified models by incorporating detailed chemical reactions, heat transfer, and material movement, using both space-based data and extensive ground-based tests. Key flight experiment goals include obtaining high-resolution measurements of droplet burning rates, flame extinction behavior, heat radiation, soot levels, and temperature profiles, while systematically analyzing the effects of different ambient conditions and suppressant concentrations. These investigations are essential for advancing the fundamental understanding of combustion processes in microgravity and enhancing fire safety protocols for future space missions.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-69_objective</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-69_hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Flame Extinguishment Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">hypothesis: The primary hypothesis of the FLEX mission is that microgravity conditions fundamentally alter the combustion and extinction characteristics of liquid fuel droplets due to changes in species and energy transport dynamics, radiative heat loss, and chemical kinetics. Specifically, the hypothesis posits that the lack of buoyant convection in microgravity will lead to significant differences in the flammability limits and suppression efficacy of various fire suppressant agents compared to normal gravity conditions. This hypothesis underpins the design of the FLEX experiments, which aim to map flammability boundaries, quantify suppressant efficacy, and develop predictive models to improve fire safety in space environments.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-69_hypothesis</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-69_research_area">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Flame Extinguishment Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">research_area: Combustion Science</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-69_research_area</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-69_research_impacts">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Flame Extinguishment Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">research_impacts: The research impacts and Earth benefits of the FLEX mission are significant and multifaceted. By enhancing our understanding of microgravity combustion, the mission directly contributes to improved fire safety protocols for future space missions, ensuring the safety of crew members in environments where traditional fire suppression methods may be ineffective. On Earth, the insights gained from these experiments have the potential to advance fire safety in confined and reduced-gravity environments, such as submarines or high-altitude aircraft. The mission's findings on the effectiveness of various fire suppressants, including CO2, He, and SF6, in microgravity can lead to the development of more efficient and less harmful fire suppression systems, reducing the risk of toxic byproducts during fire events. Additionally, the study of ''cool flames'' and low-temperature combustion processes observed in microgravity can inform the design of advanced combustion engines, leading to improved fuel efficiency and reduced emissions in automotive and industrial applications. Overall, the FLEX mission's contributions to combustion science have the potential to enhance both space exploration safety and terrestrial fire safety technologies.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-69_research_impacts</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-107_title">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">title: Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-107_title</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-107_objective">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">objective: The objective of SPICE is to better understand the complex process of combustion by observing flames in a microgravity environment aboard the International Space Station (ISS) and analogous ground experiments. Specific objectives were: (1) Identify the laminar smoke-point properties of non-buoyant jet diffusion flames under various co-flow conditions in microgravity. (2) Determine the effects of burner diameter and co-flow velocity on the luminous flame lengths and smoke points. (3) Quantify the sooting propensity of different fuels, such as ethylene, propane, and propylene mixtures. (4) Validate the correlation between luminous flame shape and residence time under microgravity conditions. (5) Investigate the impact of air and fuel flow rate variations on smoke point phenomena in space.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-107_objective</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-107_hypothesis">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">hypothesis: The research hypothesizes that by minimizing gravity-driven transport phenomena scientists can study the laminar smoke-point properties of non-buoyant jet diffusion flames. Flame properties are expected to differ significantly from those observed under normal gravity.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-107_hypothesis</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-107_research_area">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">research_area: Combustion Science</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-107_research_area</data>
</node>
<node id="FINDING::PSI-107_research_impacts">
  <data key="d0">documented_scientific_finding</data>
  <data key="d3">Smoke Point In Co-flow Experiment</data>
  <data key="d13">research_impacts: (1) Will improve our understanding of soot formation mechanisms and help design safer spacecraft environments. (2) Will contribute to refining our computational models for predicting flame behaviors under non-buoyant conditions. (3) Will enhance our knowledge of soot generation (4) Could possibly provide insights to better fuel formulations and combustion processes.</data>
  <data key="d14">PSI-107_research_impacts</data>
</node>
